regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:38:3193d56	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_005710	8 fired: 7of7 scriptable answered + opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, applied before this receipt was filed. *** THE ROUND HAD SHIPPED WITH NO PASS 2 AT ALL -- and with no CHANGELOG block, and a ledger row still reading state=held / commits="" / 'no fix exists yet' a day after the commit. The precommit gate refused the NEXT commit with 'NO PASS-2 RECEIPT for 3193d56', which is how it was caught; section F did exactly what it was built for and the round would otherwise have passed unreviewed. *** 8 seats fired, 7/7 scriptable answered (codex SHIP, agy SHIP, kimi SHIP 117 KB, grok SHIP WITH CHANGES, glm/deepseek SHIP, minimax SHIP WITH CHANGES) plus the opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED per the owner's standing deprioritisation, recorded as a deliberate omission and never as agreement. *** THE MEASURING SEAT BUILT TWELVE MUTANTS AND FIVE PASSED BOTH DETECTORS; EIGHT PASSED THE ONLY DETECTOR ANY GATE ACTUALLY RUNS. ***  The smallest is ONE TOKEN: `double bound = freq;` -> `double bound = freq / 100.0;`. At a 0.5 s accrual the shipped build owes the guest 375 interrupts and that mutant delivers FOUR -- 99% of the guest's clock destroyed, both detectors green. That is precisely the failure timer.h's 'THE BACKLOG IS RETAINED' paragraph exists to prevent. THE CAUSE WAS STRUCTURAL: R1 asserted worst_peak <= ceiling, an inequality against a CONSTANT. It computed the case's own rate `f`, PRINTED it, and compared nothing to it -- so any bound between R0's floor (4096) and the ceiling (68,157,440) satisfied it, and any bound at all satisfied the low side. CLOSED BY TWO ROWS NEEDING NO NEW ORACLE, since `f` was already computed for the message: R5 makes it an EQUALITY against the timer's own rate (kills freq/100 on 6 rows and bound-is-ceiling on 5), and R6/R6b re-arm a timer at a DIFFERENT rate and require each timer's bound to track its own (kills bound-frozen-after-first-arm and [0]-instead-of-[nr]). R6 exists because the case table ARMS ALL FOUR TIMERS WITH IDENTICAL PARAMETERS AND ARMS EACH EXACTLY ONCE, so it was structurally blind to both. Re-measured after adding them: freq/100 -> R5, bound-frozen -> R6, bound-is-ceiling -> both. The floor-deleted mutant still passes and CORRECTLY so -- byte-identical, an equivalent mutant rather than a survivor. FOUR COMMENTS IN THE SHIPPED CODE MEASURED FALSE, all corrected: (1) the clamp claimed to match timer_clamp_freq's NaN idiom and is THE INVERSE -- timer.c:80-82 puts the floor first deliberately 'so a NaN lands SLOW rather than fast' and this tested the ceiling first (harmless, NaN is unreachable: emulated_hz is an int and footbridge_effective_cycles is >= 1 by construction); (2) the floor's stated reason is false -- with it present the slowest case already reads peak 1, with it deleted the output is BYTE-IDENTICAL; (3) the ordering comment called itself load-bearing and is not -- moving the store below the switch is a ZERO-BYTE object delta passing every row, and it is redundant with the DEVINIT seed which no comment mentioned; (4) *** THE DEVINIT SEED USED THE WRONG CONSTANT *** -- it seeded the CEILING and called that 'the safe default', but at cats' 762.94 Hz the ceiling is TWENTY-FIVE HOURS of un-acked backlog and a mutant seeding it reads 3051 on the drain probe, indistinguishable from pre-fix. Now 1. A NUMBER FILED TWICE THAT THE FIX MAKES ARITHMETICALLY IMPOSSIBLE: the commit message and OUTSTANDING_BUGS both said '2772 pending after 46 s even with #442 shipped'. The bound is 762; the counter cannot exceed it. MEASURED on the shipped build: 100. The clause after it -- 'because #442 bounds the rate and not a legitimately accrued debt' -- is backwards, since pending_timer_interrupts IS the accrued debt and #442 bounds exactly that. A pre-fix projection carried into a post-fix sentence without re-measuring; corrected in the bug file and the ledger, with the same caution applied to fbdisabled's own pre-fix 3051 (shipped reads 3). The drain probe's docstring claimed 'post-fix it is the cap' -- also false: once the bound is first reached the number is a MODULAR RESIDUE of the delivered tick count (3 at 4 s, 100 at 46 s, 375 at 0.5 s, one binary, one bound), which is why --expect-cap is one-sided and cannot tell a bound of 762 from one of 7. THE WITNESS IS NOW COMMITTED as regress/fbpending_witness.py -- it lived in _scratchpad/, so a clone could not re-run the reproduction that licensed the fix (the mrwstore2 shape, whose probe left no artefact at all). Gate 6 EXPECT_CONVERTED 20 -> 21, all three counts moving together. STILL OPEN AND DECLARED: nothing runs fbpending_drain_probe.py -- gate_offline says so in a comment and gate_hygiene only COUNTS it -- and its assertion is opt-in, so wiring it in without --expect-cap would be born vacuous. Filed rather than fixed: choosing that flag's value is choosing what the row asserts, and R5/R6 now pin the property it was reaching for. Gates: gate_offline PASS (263), gate_hygiene PASS (48), each run singly.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:99:d894e35	2026-08-27	panel_20260827_062740+opus5/fable5 Agent seats	#474 footbridge TIMER_x_CLEAR read-clears; plus #473's pass-2 corrections	9of9	9 of 9 answered (kimi CLI 403-walled, kimi-k3:cloud substitute covering at 16 KB).  *** THE FABLE SEAT FOUND A FIFTH NULL-REPAIR MUTANT THAT PASSES ALL FIVE SHIPPED ROWS AND REINTRODUCES THE ROUND'S HEADLINE SYMPTOM. ***  The writeflag guard wraps THREE effects; the rows observed two.  Hoisting INTERRUPT_DEASSERT out of the guard leaves pending and timer_value untouched -- so every row passes -- while a guest READ again acknowledges a pending interrupt, which is the LOST INTERRUPT the round's own record names.  `irq_deasserts` was armed by the harness and NEVER READ: a counter nothing asserts on is a row that does not exist.  MEASURED: under the mutant all five original rows stay green and only the new row fails.  Row added in the follow-up commit (L3 -- sole carrier of the round's thesis).  codex dissented DO NOT RATIFY and was right on two measurables: the CHANGELOG still carried the BACKWARDS arithmetic the device comment had already corrected (a wrong record surviving its own fix in a sibling record), and `git diff-tree --check` reported 16 trailing-whitespace errors in the committed artifact logs.  Both fixed.  Both seats independently found the panel.sh coverage guard was HALF FIXED -- the substitute predicate moved to $miss, the CLI predicate left on $rated -- so a STALLED CLI seat fell through both branches silently.  Fixed and controlled in four states.
