regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py
  33: 
  34: VALUES, NOT SURVIVAL (rounds 79/80, and m8820x_sites_probe.py's header says the same).
  35: A row that only asks "did the host stay up" cannot tell a reachable device from an absent
  36: one -- an unmapped read survives too, it just returns 0.  So the two device rows assert
  37: values that ONLY dev_footbridge.c can produce:
  38:   * A1 reads VENDOR_ID and requires 0x1011 (dev_footbridge.c:423, DC21285_VENDOR_ID).
  39:     Nonzero and specific: the hand-assembled-encoding trap this project has been bitten by
  40:     yields 0 from a wrong register field, and 0 is the one answer this row rejects.
  41:   * A2 writes 0x12345678 to TIMER_1_LOAD and reads back 0x00345678.  THE VANISHING TOP BYTE
  42:     IS THE HANDLER'S OWN SIGNATURE: dev_footbridge.c:548 stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`
  43:     (0x00FFFFFF, dc21285reg.h:371).  RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store,
  44:     would return 0x12345678 unchanged.  This row therefore distinguishes "the device
  45:     handled it" from "the write landed somewhere", which A1 alone cannot.
  46: 
  47: THE KILL PAIR, which is this probe's own failability control.  Arms L and K differ by
  48: EXACTLY ONE INSTRUCTION.  Arm K adds a guest read of IRQ_ENABLE_SET (base+0x188), which
  49: dev_footbridge.c:495-497 answers with fatal() then exit(1) -- an UNFIXED site, used here
  50: rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless
  51: something in it can still die.  Arm L must live; arm K must exit 1 and say so.  If both
  52: survived, the guest instructions are not reaching the device at all and every SURVIVED
  53: verdict above is worthless; if both died, the probe is killing the host for its own reasons.
  54: The one-instruction delta is the entire argument.
  55: 
  56: EVERY PLANTED WORD IS CHECKED AGAINST THE DISASSEMBLY THE EMULATOR PRINTS AS IT STEPS IT --
  57: not against a separate `unassemble`, because the step line is the instruction that actually
  58: executed.  This project has a recorded incident where a wrong register field made a gate row
  59: measure the wrong thing for months.
  60: 
  61: COST, measured: see PROBE_WALL below -- 3 cold-debugger launches, no boot.  A booting-rig row
  62: was deliberately NOT written: no rig in this tree boots a footbridge machine at all, which is
  63: the gap this probe closes, and adding one would put a load-sensitive wall-clock oracle into a
  64: battery already carrying ~37.
  65: 
